### Panda Pocket - Invoice service, through the API gateway
###
### Everything here goes through the gateway on 5000 rather than hitting the
### Invoice service on 5002 directly. That is the point of a gateway: one public
### entry point, one place for authentication and rate limiting, and services
### that are not individually exposed.
###
### The status codes below are chosen deliberately. 409, 410 and 422 say three
### genuinely different things to a merchant integration, and a client can act
### on the difference: 409 means stop, 410 means get a fresh invoice, 422 means
### the customer still owes money.

@gw = http://localhost:5000
@invoice = http://localhost:5002

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Create an invoice. 201.
###
### Invoice calls Rate for a quote, fixes that rate on the invoice, and never
### recalculates it. That single decision is the product: the merchant is quoted
### R250 and receives R250 no matter what Bitcoin does over the next fifteen
### minutes, because the platform carries the price risk, not the shop.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json
X-Correlation-Id: demo-create-001

{
  "amountZar": 250.00,
  "reference": "COFFEE-SHOP-0001",
  "asset": "BTC"
}

### Validation failure. 400 with the specific offending fields, which is far
### more use to an integrator than a bare "bad request".
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amountZar": -5,
  "reference": "",
  "asset": "BTC"
}

### Same reference twice. 409.
### The unique index on (merchant_id, reference) is what stops a merchant's own
### retry logic quietly creating two invoices for one customer order.
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amountZar": 250.00,
  "reference": "COFFEE-SHOP-0001",
  "asset": "BTC"
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Read and list
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Replace the id with one returned above.
GET {{gw}}/api/invoices/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### List. Paging parameters are optional and default to page 1, size 20.
GET {{gw}}/api/invoices

###
GET {{gw}}/api/invoices?status=Pending&pageSize=10

### An unrecognised status returns 400 listing the valid values, rather than
### silently returning an empty list and letting the caller think there is no data.
GET {{gw}}/api/invoices?status=Bogus

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Payments
###
### POST .../payments stands in for a blockchain confirmation. In a real gateway
### this is called by a chain watcher, not by the customer.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Underpayment. 422.
### The payment is real and is stored, so the invoice moves to Underpaid and can
### still be topped up. 422 rather than 400 because the request was perfectly
### well formed; it just does not satisfy the invoice.
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/payments
Content-Type: application/json
X-Correlation-Id: demo-create-001

{
  "txHash": "tx-demo-underpay-01",
  "amountCrypto": 0.00001
}

### Replay the same txHash. 409.
### Rejected by the unique constraint on payments.tx_hash, in the database
### rather than in application code. That matters: an application-level check
### can race with itself under concurrent requests, a unique index cannot. The
### same one line of DDL is both the idempotency guard, so a duplicated
### confirmation cannot credit a merchant twice, and the replay detector.
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/payments
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "txHash": "tx-demo-underpay-01",
  "amountCrypto": 0.00001
}

### Top up to the full amount. 200, and the invoice becomes Paid.
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/payments
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "txHash": "tx-demo-topup-01",
  "amountCrypto": 0.00014
}

### Payment against an expired invoice. 410 Gone.
### Not 404, because the invoice exists, and not 400, because the request is
### valid. What changed is that the window closed and the locked rate is no
### longer one the platform will honour. Gone tells the integration to request a
### fresh invoice rather than retry this one.
###
### To see it: create an invoice, wait for the expiry window, then pay it. Drop
### Invoice__ExpiryMinutes to 1 in docker-compose.yml to avoid waiting fifteen.
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/payments
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "txHash": "tx-demo-late-01",
  "amountCrypto": 0.00014
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Cancel
###
### An action endpoint, not a PATCH on the status field. A state transition is
### not a field edit: exposing status as writable would let any client set any
### value it liked, including Settled, which would mean claiming money had been
### paid out. Stripe uses the same shape with POST /v1/invoices/{id}/void.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/cancel
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reason": "Customer changed their mind"
}

### Cancelling something already terminal returns 409. Every transition is
### checked against the shared state machine in Shared/Contracts, so the rule is
### stated once and cannot drift between services.
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/cancel
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Audit trail
###
### One row per transition, written in the same SaveChanges as the transition
### itself, so the history cannot drift from the invoice it describes.
###
### This table is three things at once: the audit trail, the SOC event source,
### and the edge source for the Neo4j knowledge graph in the next phase, where
### each row becomes a timestamped edge carrying the correlation id that links
### it to everything else in the same request.
###
### Note that sweeper-driven expiries have a null correlation id. That is
### deliberate and honest: no caller's request caused them, time did.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{gw}}/api/invoices/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/history

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Tracing
###
### Supply a correlation id and the gateway forwards it to every service. Filter
### Seq at http://localhost:5341 by
###
###   CorrelationId = 'demo-create-001'
###
### and one payment is visible crossing Gateway, Invoice and Rate in order, with
### the SOC events alongside. Omit the header and the gateway mints one, echoed
### back on the response so it can be quoted.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Health. The gateway reports only its own liveness on purpose: a gateway that
### called itself unhealthy because one service was down would be pulled out of
### rotation exactly when it could still route to the others.
GET {{gw}}/health

### Invoice's own health probes Postgres rather than just confirming the process
### is running.
GET {{invoice}}/health

### OpenAPI. Swagger UI is at {{invoice}}/swagger.
GET {{invoice}}/swagger/v1/swagger.json
